 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Precision**: The assertion `farPtr < _shortPtrTop` requires careful management of `_shortPtrTop` [S1, unofficial]. If the tree is very deep or sparse, far pointers may be needed frequently [S1, unofficial].
-- **Boundaries**: The `pos` variable must be carefully managed to avoid buffer overflows [S1, unofficial]. The source uses `dataSize - pos` to index backwards, ensuring that children are written before parents in the contiguous array [S1, unofficial].
-- **Degenerate Input**: If the input tree is empty, `dataSize` will be small, and the algorithm should handle it gracefully [S1, unofficial].
+- **Precision**: The relative pointer arithmetic relies on `pos` and `childAdr` being consistent. Off-by-one errors in `pos` initialization or incrementation will corrupt the tree structure [S1, unofficial].
+- **Boundaries**: The `_shortPtrTop` threshold must be chosen carefully. If set too low, excessive far pointers are allocated, increasing memory usage. If set too high, relative offsets may overflow the 15-bit field [S1, unofficial].
+- **Degenerate Input**: Empty volumes result in `NULL` roots, which must be handled by the caller before compaction [S1, unofficial].
