+### Min/Max Tree Construction for Ray Tracing
+This variant extends the compacted octree with a min/max tree to accelerate ray tracing by culling empty space. It differs from the base compaction by computing bounding volumes for nodes based on forward-adjacent voxels, not just the node's own children [S3, unofficial]. It is chosen when ray tracing performance is critical and the scene has large empty regions. The storage overhead is approximately 22% additional footprint on top of the raw scalar octree-compressed data [S3, unofficial]. Construction involves computing min/max pairs for leaf nodes based on cell corner values and forward neighbors, then recursively computing parent min/max pairs from children [S3, unofficial]. Leaf nodes may omit min/max pairs to save storage, forcing neighbor-finding at runtime [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of nodes in the octree [G]. Two passes are made: one for allocation/offset calculation and one for writing [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the compacted array [G]. The original pointer-based tree is typically freed after compaction [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: High spatial locality, reduced memory overhead (no pointers per node, just offsets), cache-friendly traversal [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Static structure; inserting or deleting nodes requires full or partial recompaction [G]. The `_shortPtrTop` parameter may lead to inefficient far pointer usage if not tuned [S1, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity**: Construction is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of logical nodes, as each node is visited a constant number of times during allocation and compaction [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: The compacted layout reduces pointer overhead significantly compared to a standard pointer-based tree. The exact compression ratio depends on the tree balance and the `_shortPtrTop` threshold [S1, unofficial].
+- **Performance**: Provides speedups of 25% to 45% when combined with smaller kernels in splatting methods by reducing the cost of handling invisible material [S2].
+- **Strengths**: Cache-friendly layout, reduced memory footprint, fast traversal due to contiguous memory.
+- **Limitations**: Requires two-pass construction (logical build then compact). Far pointers add complexity to traversal logic.
 
 ## Background
-The implementation follows the compact octree layout and allocation routines found in the `svo-workbench` repository, which also includes a `readFromBinvox_rec` function for loading voxel data [S1, unofficial]. It is commonly used in real-time volume rendering and ray tracing where memory bandwidth and cache efficiency are critical [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the "svo-workbench" project, an unofficial implementation of sparse voxel octree rendering techniques [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to address the high cost of handling invisible material in splatting and raycasting by using compacted octree encoding alongside occlusion culling [S2]. The approach builds on earlier work in volume rendering evaluation, which highlighted the need for efficient sparse structures [S2].
 
